hi my name is jessica boyd i am a  clinical biochemist at alberta precision  laboratories in calgary and i'm also  a clinical associate professor at the  cummings school of medicine at the  university of calgary  today i'm going to be giving a  presentation on laboratory testing for  opioids  with some discussion on uh  interpretation and different  technologies and then how we can use  some data mining techniques to help  customize panels for  clinics in your area  so just briefly my objectives as i  mentioned are disc to discuss  the clinical laboratory and the impact  that the opioid crisis has had  on us as lab professionals discuss the  important factors in setting up a urine  drug screening program  with emphasis on the opioids and then  compare  the available techniques for urine drug  screening for opioids and the challenges  that though each of those technologies  uh presentation  so of course the opioids are very  diverse class of drugs that are used  clinically for analgesia and for opioid  agonist therapy  they are um anything that's an opioid  can  is something that can interact with the  opioid receptors in the nervous system  and they come in three  kind of flavors new kappa and delta and  depending on those drugs interaction  with those receptors you can have  different  impacts different types of effects  of course we know that people who come  in with acute opioid poisoning will  present with a particular toxidrome  some of the features that i've listed  below but of course we  know that in high enough doses um that  type of poisoning is fatal and that is  really what has precipitated  the opioid crisis that we're seeing  today in  especially in north america and where i  am in canada  of course this story started three or  four years ago  when particularly in alberta here for  example we started to see  things like this show up in our illicit  drug supply so on the left-hand picture  we have  a picture of what a normal oxycontin 80  milligram  uh tablet would look like and you can  see it's green it has an 80  uh stamped onto one side or at least  these are the ones that we get anyway  and what we started to see in alberta  and what was being seized by  our local police forces are is shown in  the left-hand  picture which are very similar looking  tablets  they have the same kind of stamping the  same coloration  um the same look to them but they don't  have oxycodone in them  they have fentanyl and fentanyl is much  more potent  and so this was leading to a large  number of people  unfortunately passing away because they  were taking a drug that they weren't  anticipating in their  in their drug supply and so this has  been  the opioid crisis has hit us and canada  very hard and i'm going to show some  statistics  of what opioid poisoning hospitalization  rates have looked like this is data from  2017 but this certainly has not resolved  uh in the past three years and you can  see that this has touched every corner  of our country and it has touched other  countries as well i know the united  states in particular  where i am in alberta which is towards  the western side of the country  we have seen um particularly  high rates of hospitalization vancouver  in bc  has kind of been the epicenter for  canada for this crisis  and that's definitely reflected in these  in these hospitalization  rates and if we were to dig down into  these numbers you would see that this is  also something that's predominantly  affecting  young people so people in the 15 to 40  age group so of course one of the um  big pushes for um the last five years  has been to try  and get the uh identify these  individuals  and get them into a treatment program  and in the province of alberta  our provincial government has um has put  significant amount of resources into  increasing the number of  treatment spots in opioid dependency  programs  and so that has had a very positive  effect  and one of the side effects i guess of  that is that we are seeing  increases in prescribing of drugs like  buprenorphine or suboxone  or methadone as these patients enter  these treatment programs and so this  graph is showing  a steady increase in suboxone dispensing  in alberta and this is a trend that  is continuing into the into 2020  and this is probably what has a bigger  impact on  laboratory testing because of course  these patients are typically  monitored through urine drug screening  to ensure compliance as part of their  program  so in terms of the laboratory impacts  from the opioid crisis  and these are ones that that we felt  here in alberta but i know that are not  exclusive to us in alberta  is we saw huge increase in requests for  urine drug screening starting in about  2015 2016  and i'll show you some data later about  what exactly that looks like and  and show you some of the strategies we  took to try and address that  considering that we're unsure we're all  in a resource finite  environment and there isn't money  necessarily to hire  a whole bunch of new staff or always get  new instruments all the time  but it wasn't just an increase in the  number of drug tests that we were  receiving we were also getting requests  for certain types of tests within the  panel  so of course one of those was fentanyl  prior to 2015 we did not have fentanyl  on our amino acid panels and we probably  honestly didn't think we were ever going  to have to really do it  in large volumes because um fentanyl was  fairly difficult to get a hold of you  had to get it as part of a fentanyl  patch  or diversion from hospital supplies and  so it wasn't as  readily available of course as soon as  some  powders started coming through the mail  containing fentanyl that  definitely changed the situation in  terms of what we needed to do  one of the other big changes was  requests for buprenorphine  testing so again four or five years ago  i would say 98 of our opioid dependency  treatment patients were on methadone  and the switch to buprenorphine has  happened over the last  few years as we try to again increase  treatment spaces um increase the number  of providers who can actually prescribe  this drug get people started in the  emergency department when they're  presenting um and so  now if we were to look at at our data um  i i we have actually more patients on  buprenorphine than we do methadone  so um that was also a major change that  we had to deal with fairly quickly  in 2015-16  so it's always good when we're talking  about setting up or changing a urine  drug screen program  to have an appreciation for what kind of  patient population we're looking at  and of course with the opioids there's  several different areas that this can  touch  so um typically with opioids i'm kind of  thinking of three main areas  for my local area one is emergency  departments  and this is one that often comes up um  in  the news or the literature about you  know what do we do for them  and of course they're the ones seeing  patients presenting for acute opioid  poisoning  and i think it's really important to  remember that um  you know while they may order a urine  drug screen  they're actually um not waiting for the  result of that or they shouldn't be  to be prescribing or administering  naloxone if  someone is showing up with an opioid  toxidrome  and so in these cases the urine drug  screen may be helpful  later to decide what exactly the patient  came in on  but it's not actually particularly  helpful for acute management  and so um you know we're not  necessarily setting up a program to get  results back right away  they've got an antidote and they should  be giving that so where we've really put  our time and our energy  is looking at what the needs of for  example our pain management programs are  but particularly  in my area what the needs of our opioid  dependency treatment programs are  because that's where  people are are moving to hopefully once  they've presented to the ed  the needs of a pain management and  opioid dependency treatment program are  a little bit different than of course  the ed because typically  what they're doing is checking  compliance with prescribed medications  those prescribed medications are not  necessarily the same between those two  groups  of course for opioid dependency programs  uh buprenorphine  and methadone are probably two important  ones  but your pain management providers may  be using a whole variety of things and  it's important to understand  what they're using so that those can be  on your panel and of course  we always usually have some of the other  commonly used drugs or drug classes so  then those are amphetamines or what not  because we're also looking to make sure  that they're not  taking other things perhaps in addition  to their  prescriptions  so in terms of the laboratory process  for drugs with abuse testing this is  a pretty standard process used by many  labs  um and it's usually a two-step process  where we have some sort of screen  using an amino acid um  and then depending on some sort of  criteria sometimes it's  you know all positive sometimes it's  certain positives  then they go on for confirmation testing  by mass spectrometry  in my lab we have both of these  technologies so we're able to control  in-house what goes where i know there  are many areas that do not have  mass spectrometry on site and it's an  expensive referral test and so  controlling what goes out for mass spec  is  is more of an issue but i i'm hoping to  show you by the end of this  talk why having mass spectrometry  available for the opioids  um is important um due to the the  limitations of immunoassay screening and  which is really  it really is a screen and it is not a  definitive result  so of course amino acids are present uh  in many places and as i mentioned that  they may be the only  type of uh testing available on site  and there's reasons for this they are  pretty simple to perform  you can get the result pretty quickly  and they can be performed at the point  of collection and there are  point-of-care programs set up for these  but  it is really important to remember that  amino assays  are prone to false positives they're  prone to false negatives  and within that test itself it cannot  tell you  if that is a false negative or a false  positive we just know that there was  reactivity with an  antibody within the test and so these  results  require confirmation by mass  spectrometry  um and um otherwise you can't tell  whether this was something actually an  opioid  in some of these tests which opioid um  or whether it was something that's not  an opioid but just happens to  cross-react with the antibody so this is  the real strength of mass spectrometry  for  this type of testing  so i just want to take a few minutes and  talk a little bit about opioid  versus opiate nomenclature because this  is also very important for understanding  the interpretation of these tests  often opioid and opiate are used  interchangeably but they are not the  same thing and in this table  i've shown a kind of an overview of how  they  how they work together so opioid is  an umbrella term that refers to anything  that can  interact with the opioid receptor  essentially um  and so everything in this table and like  i said this isn't an  exhaustive table but all of these would  be opioids  the opiates are in the left-hand column  and those uh typically refer to the  opioids that can be derived from the  opium poppy so we're talking primarily  about morphine and codeine  and i've put a structure down at the  bottom so that you can see  that they have a five ring structure  with different side groups at kind of  three different places on the backbone  that change whether it's you know  morphine coating or metabolite  in the middle column we have the  semisynthetic opioids  and i've thrown things like hydrocodone  hydromorphone in here  now hydrocodone hydromorphone are  metabolites of morphine and codeine  but when we're talking about urine drug  screening  um we know that actually despite being  metabolites  of morphine codeine they don't actually  react  as well as morphine encoding in the  urine opiates amino acid  um in fact in our assay locally for  example  uh we get about 50 reactivity meaning  that we need  twice as much of hydrocodone and  hydromorphone  in the urine to get the same response  as morphine and codeine and so that's a  big source of false negative results and  why  at my institution if if my staff see  that a patient is on one of these  we actually just send it straight to  mass effect  i put oxycodone here as well oxycodone  may or may not react with your urine  opiates assay and ours it doesn't react  at all  but again if the assumption is that a  urine ops assay reacts with  all opioids and it's not understood that  it really is just  picking up morphine and codeine um then  that can be  a perceived i guess false negative and  then in the right hand column we have  the synthetic opioids  this includes things like methadone  fentanyl buprenorphine  and these ones really there is no  reactivity in the urine opiates assay  at all and you need to have a separate  amino acid for testing for these  and you can see at the bottom um the  structures are quite different they  don't even  have that fibrin backbone anymore and so  that kind of  you know visually can help make help us  understand why that antibody may not  pick that up  metabolism is also really important to  understand for urine drug screens  because in urine  typically it's the metabolite that we're  seeing at the highest concentration not  the parent  and so one of the most common calls i  get is understanding how  codeine morphine and heroin and their  various metabolites  go together on our drug screen panels so  in the middle  you can see codeine and morphine here  um codeine of course is a pro drug  morphine is  is what we want it to metabolize to once  you've taken it  um i've already mentioned that both of  those can also be metabolized to  hydrocodone and hydromorphone those are  at the top of the screen  they're minor metabolites but they are  active but they can also be given as  drugs themselves  towards the bottom of the screen we have  the inactive metabolites and the  glucuroni  um so nor morphine and then glucuronide  glucoronidated forms and then on the  right upper  side of this diagram you see where  heroin can  fit into this uh into this pathway and  it's  heroin and will metabolize the six  monoacetyl morphine or six mam  which then metabolizes to morphine so  in a urine opiate assay i've already  told you that it will pick up  primarily codeine and morphine but  depending on your assay  you may have cross reactivity with six  monoacetylmorphine or heroin  um and for the assay we use locally does  pick both of those up  so this is another caution about acting  on a urine opiates result  because if it's positive even if it's a  true positive  you don't know whether that positive is  coming from codeine presence  morphine being present or heroin and its  metabolite being present potentially  so again why mass spectrometry is really  important so that we can speciate those  out  and give that information to the  physician  this is just a simple diagram for some  of the other  opioids that we're typically looking at  they're a little bit more simple than  the codeine morphine  group um so typically we're looking at  you know one uh parent one metabolite  pair  um you can see that buprenorphine also  has some glucoronides floating around  there  um and here um just would point out  that for methadone and buprenorphine we  actually  monitor even on our immunoassays at  least for methadone  both the parent and the metabolite and  the reason that we do that  is because we're trying to pick up  people who might be adding  some of their methadone or buprenorphine  to their urine an attempt to fool the  drug screen  um the drug screen i guess isn't being  fooled there would be actually the  methadone in the urine  but if it's present without the  metabolite or without  um significant amounts of metabolite it  suggests that there was some  adulteration of the urine and so that's  a practice that we've done for quite a  long time locally  for our opioid dependency clinics  so what immunoassays are available for  testing of opioids and urine so i've  i've made a list here this is not fully  exhaustive but it's  there to give you an idea that there are  lots of options and you could put a  whole bunch of opiate amino acids on  your wet or your chemistry analyzer  but it's going to start taking up a lot  of room at some point and so this is  again  back to knowing what your population is  and knowing which of these tests are  actually useful  to have on your instrument  it is really important to note here that  cross your activity is going to vary  between the vendors  so make sure you take a look at the  package insert and understand  um you know what your opiates assay is  it reacting with heroin and system and  particularly well is it not  if it's not you may need to have a  separate heroin metabolite assay  um what's your cross reactivity with  hydrocortisone hydromorphone or do you  need to look at having a separate  immunoassay for that  um another thing to pay attention to are  cut  the opac for example usually comes with  two different cutoffs  a clinical and  workplace cut off and you what you pick  may depend again on your population and  maybe also on your resources for sending  out from aspect if you don't have it on  on site so this is understanding your  reflex  algorithm may also play a role in that  as well  now because of all of the assays that we  would potentially be looking at to put  on our instruments  and the fact that we really don't at my  institution didn't have room to put all  of these on and that these are all still  even if they're targeting you know  hydrocodone or fentanyl specifically  that amino acids are still not  definitive results but  so the decision that we did at our  institution was to start moving all of  this onto mass spec  um to kind of bypass all the  interpretive issues that we see with  with immunoassays and also the amount of  testing that we would need  um and so we are in the process of like  i said of discontinuing most of our  immunoassays in favor of mass  spectrometry  and the way that we've decided to do  this is through  liquid chromatography tandem mass  spectrometry  using targeted analysis with mrm uh  multiple reaction monitoring scans so  this picture in the middle here is a  picture of a triple quadrupole  uh and it is uh in mrm um  mrm mode and if you don't know what that  is essentially um  what we do in the q1 box is we tell the  instrument to only allow the ions of a  certain  molecular um sorry charge  mass to charge ratio um so for example  we should know as part of method  development what the mass to charge  ratio of morphine would be  so we'll tell the instrument only allow  the morphine mass to charge ratio  through  now of course there is a possibility  that there is another molecule in that  sample that has the same  mass to charge as morphine floating  around if you've done your  chromatography well  you may have separated it already but  in case you didn't the mass spec allows  you another  opportunity to filter that out then it  does that  by fragmenting that massive charge in q2  to produce product ions and then again  as part of our method development we  would have identified the major product  ions of morphine  and told the instrument okay these are  the top two or three product ions of  morphine  and we only want that to transit to the  detector at any given time and so that  will give us our mrn signal  on the right hand side and so typically  in any one of our drug screen  analyses we'd be monitoring at least two  of these transitions per analyte  um and we are able to do this  simultaneously so that we can look at  all of these opioids  you know and other classes of drugs at  the same time  the downside of this of course is that  um  if there is something around in in the  urine  that is not on your list that you've  pre-defined with the instrument you're  not going to see it  um and so this is um this is definitely  a downside in that  picking up new drug trends is difficult  with analyzing your samples as well  but we made the decision to go this way  because we had so many samples that we  um  we needed to get through that it was  more important to kind of look for the  the key ones and then we would kind of  do work with our partners  in the province to work on identifying  uh new drugs that we  may need to add the other advantage of  mass spectrometry  particularly if you're doing it as a lab  develop test or an ldt  is that you can add new analytes  yourself  it does require method development and  you may need to go muck around with your  with your method to get it to work  but typically you can add those new  analytes if you can find a standard  a lot sooner than it would take for a  new immunoassay test to be developed  um if you've been paying attention to  the news you know we've seen over the  last five years  you know several new um drugs kind of  start to show up in the drug supply  along with fentanyl carfentanyl as an  example  and that's one that we added to our mass  spec panels  um and i don't um i think i've only seen  one or two little point of care  devices so far for carfentanil i'm sure  people are working on it so  you you can potentially move a bit  faster on some of these new emerging  drugs as well  so just to give you an example of what  one of our opioid panels looks like  um this has all of our kind of  important parents and metabolites from  codeine  and morphine metabolism we have  methadone and  dpp buprenorphine or buprenorphine  oxycodone or oxycodone fentanyl nor  fentanyl  and so the real power here is that  you're able to monitor for all of these  simultaneously  in one sample and so i can look at it  and go  um are the are the coding  or the what we see in terms of morphine  coding nor coding  does that look like a codeine pattern  does it look like maybe they're taking  hydrocodone hydromorphone separately  um and so it gives me a lot more  information than if i'm just trying to  decipher  an amino acid result for all of these  so in the last few minutes i just wanted  to show you some of what we've done in  calgary to try and address  uh the our our issues with the opioid  epidemic  um you can see here a graph of  our workload volumes and requests for  urine drug screens  between 2010 and 2017 and you can see  that  you know prior to 2015 we had pretty  stable  direct screen requests it was it was  pretty much the same number plus or  minus  you know five percent very predictable  we had totally  arranged our staffing and our  instrumentation usage around it because  you know we didn't have any major  changes  you can see in 2012 we did see an  increase but that's because we had a new  clinic coming on board and we knew that  was happening so it actually made  um it was fairly easy to plan um like i  said our staffing  and workflow around this but in 2015  but particularly 2016 and 17 as fentanyl  started to show up  in the drug supply in alberta we started  to see  those numbers shoot way up and we  actually saw a doubling  of our volume over two or three years  which  really threw our entire process out of  whack  we were not able to keep up with the  samples coming in  we had to start doing elaborate sorting  processes  to make sure that we were getting kind  of ed  and hospital samples out and we would do  opioid dependency samples later because  that would take us probably two or three  weeks  at one point it was taking us seven or  eight weeks to actually get those  results out which is starting to impact  the match in both patients as well  and so we knew our process was not  sustainable needed some major changes  and so we went to the drawing board to  kind of decide what we were going to do  and part of  the decision at that time was to start  moving  all of our samples to a fully lcm sms  process a targeted panel so we would you  know  have you know 20 or 30 drugs on that  panel  we would use  automated liquid handling to extract our  samples which we were doing manually  before then and then we would  get middleware to connect our  instruments to the lif so that  we didn't have someone manually putting  results in  as we were doing in 2014  but part of the big question there was  well what  what do we need on these panels and and  um  are we going to have one panel for  everybody or are we going to have  customized for certain user groups  within our population because we do  we do all the urine drug screening in  southern alberta so we cover  everybody and so what we did there  to address that question was to do a  retrospective data analysis of our urine  drug screens  to see who is ordering them what drugs  are we seeing commonly in our population  and i've got some of the summary  information here  we did just over 50 000 drug screens  over a four year period  that represented just over 12 000  individual patients  and on average we were seeing four uh  just over four  drug screens per patient over that time  period  um but of course we had quite a big  range on that you know majority of those  patients were coming in with just one  drug screen because they may have  against emergency department or  something but then we would have  a large cohort of our our patients who  were  opioid dependency program members and so  we're coming in  regularly to have samples and so we had  one individual over that four  year period who had over 100 drug  screens  so definitely showed us that we had  different groups within our population  so who ordered the most drug screens  when we broke it down by  ordering provider it was very clear and  and i think we knew this before  that um addictions medicine  was the number one requester and the  majority of those  were opioid dependency clinics and so  we wanted to definitely work with that  group to figure out  how this process was going to work  because they were also represented most  of our repeat  customers  so one of the biggest users within the  addictions medicine group was physician  a  uh this was the sole physician at the  largest methadone clinic in calgary  he had actually kind of spearheaded a  lot of the methadone treatment  more than 10 years before and so  um was very knowledgeable and had been  around for a long time and some new  patients really well  and he actually represented he his  patients  cohort represented a quarter of our  total workload so obviously getting him  on board with any changes we were doing  was  was extremely important  so um i did a as part of a data poll i  looked at what  drugs we were detecting within his  population and so it's not really  surprised that  majority majority of the positive drugs  in his population was methadone and at  the time as i mentioned earlier  methadone was the primary drug  um being used for opioid dependency at  that time  and you can see that 88 of the  assessments received from that  that clinic population were positive um  then looking down the rest of this list  and this is just kind of the top  grouping we see morphine codeine  diphenhydramine ibuprofen oxycodone  and so we wanted but the the numbers are  dropping off in terms of percent  positive  quite rapidly and so um you know i had a  very long list of things that you know  were less than one percent positive and  so we wanted to make sure we were  pulling out kind of the  the really um relevant most commonly  detected drugs  out of his uh population for a panel  um so what i proposed to him was this  panel  which was an lcms targeted panel  that had of course methadone edp at that  time  his clinic wasn't prescribing  buprenorphine but we did add  buprenorphine  later we have morphine codeine 6-man  oxycodone and metabolites cocaine  fentanyl nor fentanyl acetylene  methamphetamine  and so we went and met with him and said  what do you think these are the issues  that we're having  moving to this panel would allow us to  give you your results in a  less than one week time frame as opposed  to the seven or eight week  time frame that we were dealing with at  the time  and so we had a really good we had a  really good discussion with him  he um he definitely understood the  problems that we were facing and he was  definitely motivated  to help us find a way to get his samples  back  to him sooner he was also um  one of the big advocates for making sure  that they could have access to lcms  because he understood  all of the issues with the immunoassay  interpretation  um and so he actually wanted all of his  patients to go to mass spec if we could  accommodate that and so this was one of  the ways that we said that we could  potentially do that  we also discussed the data pool that we  had done and  presented the proposed lcms panel and  had a really good discussion with him  about it  um and so after that discussion we ended  up expanding the panel slightly to 21  drugs we kept those  the opioids amphetamines we added a few  benzodiazepines that he had said he was  having  problems with from his population  although  you know we're looking at the drug  screen side and saying that you know  they're not detected often but you know  it's the feedback of what  the physician is getting from his  patients or her patients is really  important as well  we kept cocaine and added a few  adulterants that were commonly seen in  our population  we added a urine ethanol screen and some  antidepressants so  those are the ones that he felt were  really important for his  for helping him to manage his patient  and moving to 21 drugs on that panel  um wasn't too onerous on our side to  make sure that we were able to actually  move to this process  and discontinue kind of our old process  that was  that was really hindering us at that  point  so we did a similar process with the  other seven  opioid dependency clinics that we had at  that time  and so all of them actually had pretty  much this  a very similar backbone of um a panel  and then  we did a few kind of customizations  depending on what they were seeing  particularly in their population  um as i mentioned we added automation  steps for this  as well so that we had a robot doing a  sample prep and we interfaced  directly to the lif so that results  could be released directly  and so now uh we can prepare analyze and  result  multiple 96 well plates per day if if  needed  and we have capacity uh to handle  further increases in our workload volume  which is really important  with our old process i should mention  that we probably on a good day  and if we had our really experienced  technologist doing the  data interpretation we could maybe get  20 to 30 samples out  so this represents a significant  increase  in our throughput and over the past um  two or three years um we really haven't  had any issues with samples piling up  because this process is able to allow us  to  get those samples out quickly and it  allows us to give an lc an sms result  directly to our clinicians which i think  they appreciate very much as well  so in summary the opioid epidemic has  had  significant impacts on health care  including laboratory medicine  particularly for us for urine drug  screening testing  immunoassay screens are convenient but  they  have major issues in terms of false  positives and false negatives  um and so mass spectrometry is really  such a useful tool for allowing us to  monitor parents and metabolites in a  sample to speciate those all  out individually and give us a  definitive result  and so if you have the opportunity to do  mass spec  on these samples it's i think it really  does provide  much better information on amino acid  drug screening  and uh finally retrospective data mining  can help  help you guide your drug screen panel  development if you if you've got that  information  and help open that conversation with  your physicians about  what might be appropriate for their for  their clinics  so in conclusion or acknowledgement i'd  like to acknowledge  dr hussain savaday who is my co-director  for the analytical toxicology department  and also the section chief of  biochemistry  at apl calgary uh dr chris knogler  and of course all of my colleagues in  the clinical biochemistry department in  calgary  and with that i'm happy to take any  questions  that will be forwarded to me thank you  very much
 

